NCAR/SPERR source mirror, revision b8012586908aaabba993038d183b3937c8ea9c7c.
https://github.com/NCAR/SPERR

include/, src/, LICENSE and SperrConfig.h.in come from the pinned upstream revision.
SPECK_INT.cpp and SPECK_FLT.cpp have a 32-bit portability patch: compare stream
lengths in uint64_t before converting the bounded minimum to size_t.
Bitstream.cpp restricts the 64-bit streaming store to x86-64; 32-bit x86 uses
the existing scalar store.
../sperr.cmake builds only the 2D codec, without OpenMP, CLI, tests, installation,
shared libraries, LTO or architecture-specific flags. It replaces upstream build
integration, which assumes CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR is the SPERR source directory.
The build supplies <algorithm>, missing from upstream Bitstream.cpp on libc++.
On Android it stages sperr_helper.cpp with posix_memalign in place of aligned_alloc
(unavailable before API 28). The adaptation is isolated in ../sperr.cmake and
../sperr_android_helper.cpp.in; the pinned file remains unchanged.

Terrain profile 1 uses the headerless 2D pointwise-error bitstream. The private
C++ wrapper calls the same SPECK2D_FLT operations as the C API, checks both decode
statuses, and owns the decoded vector directly (avoiding the C API's extra copy).
Client code uses decompression only. Do not upgrade without comparing the frozen
integer reconstruction fixtures on all supported architectures.
